\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A convergent expansion over closed walks}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let the vertices be
#{\Lambda=\prod_{j=1}^d\mathbb Z/L_j\mathbb Z}, with #{L_j\ge3}.
Give each edge #{(x,j):x\to x+e_j} weight one and a unitary or
orthogonal transport #{U_{x,j}} on a fixed fiber. For the
\vocabk{determinant action}{fdip-0070}, put #{D=2d+m^2}, where #{m>0},
and let #{H_U} be the covariant adjacency operator. Then
##{Q_U=DI-H_U,\qquad \|H_U\|\le2d<D,}
and
##{W(U)=\kappa N\log D-
 \kappa\sum_{k\ge1}\frac{\operatorname{tr}_{\mathbb K}(H_U^k)}{kD^k}.}
The series is absolutely convergent, uniformly over all such unitary
connections. For #{K\ge0}, its remainder after length #{K} satisfies
##{|R_K(U)|\le\frac{\kappa N}{K+1}
 \frac{(2d/D)^{K+1}}{1-2d/D}.}
Each trace is the sum of fiber holonomy traces over all based oriented
closed nearest-neighbor walks of length #{k}.}

\proof{
\p{Define the covariant shift
##{(T_j\phi)(x)=U_{x-e_j,j}\phi(x-e_j).}
A permutation of sites and the unitary fiber maps preserve the norm,
so #{T_j} is unitary. Expanding the difference energy gives
##{H_U=\sum_{j=1}^d(T_j+T_j^*),\qquad Q_U=(2d+m^2)I-H_U.}
Thus #{H_U} is self-adjoint and the triangle inequality gives
#{\|H_U\|\le2d}. If its eigenvalues are #{h_1,\ldots,h_N},
then #{|h_i|/D<1}. Apply the scalar Taylor series for
#{\log(1-h_i/D)} to
#{\log\det Q_U=N\log D+\sum_i\log(1-h_i/D)}.
There are finitely many eigenvalues and
##{|\operatorname{tr}(H_U^k)|\le N(2d)^k,}
so the resulting trace series is absolutely convergent. For
#{k\ge K+1}, bound #{1/k\le 1/(K+1)} and sum the geometric tail.
This proves the stated uniform remainder.}

\p{Choose an ordered orthonormal basis of the fixed fiber. In
that basis, and in the vertex decomposition of #{\mathcal H},
the matrix block of #{H_U} from #{s(e)} to #{h(e)} is #{U_e},
and the reverse block is #{U_e^*}. Multiplying #{k} blocks follows
an oriented walk; the diagonal blocks are exactly the closed walks.
The ordered product agrees with the
[transport convention](fdip-006N), and taking the fiber trace gives
the claimed expansion. Reverse walks pair adjoint products, so the
total is real even for a complex fiber.}
}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{Real.hasSum_pow_div_log_of_abs_lt_one} states, for a real
#{x} with #{|x|<1},
##{\sum_{n\ge0}\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}=-\log(1-x).}
It supplies the scalar series used for each real eigenvalue. Spectral
reduction, the walk interpretation, the lattice norm bound and the
uniform trace remainder are the additional arguments above; the
formal declaration does not state this lattice expansion.}

\p{The numerical bound #{2d} and scalar diagonal #{D} use this
regular lattice and its unit edge weights. For arbitrary weights or
degrees one must change the diagonal decomposition and norm estimate;
the displayed bound is not asserted unchanged.}

\p{Kazakov and Migdal integrate an adjoint matrix field in a different
lattice model. Their Section 2, equations (3)–(4), gives a trace-log
and loop weights involving #{|\operatorname{tr}U[\Gamma]|^2}
\citet{p. 2}{kazakov1992induced}. This is a comparison with the
closed-walk method. The finite real-scalar coefficient below follows
from the present proof, not from their large-#{N} analysis or a
continuum identification.}
